<h2>What is a normal HRV for my age?</h2>
<p><strong>There is no single normal HRV range that applies to every person of the same age.</strong> A useful reference must match the metric, recording length and measurement conditions. A brief Apple Watch reading, an overnight average and a 24-hour ECG result should not be treated as interchangeable.</p>
<p><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5624990/">Shaffer and Ginsberg's review of HRV metrics and norms</a> explains why age, recording duration and the study population affect reference values. Age-related patterns in a population do not create a diagnostic cutoff for your Watch reading.</p>
<h2>Before using an HRV-by-age chart</h2>
<table><caption>Check that a reference actually matches your measurement</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Metric: SDNN or RMSSD</td><td>Both use milliseconds, but summarize beat-to-beat variation differently.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recording duration</td><td>A short sample cannot be interpreted using a 24-hour reference range.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Resting, exercising, sleeping and paced breathing are different contexts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Source population</td><td>A study's participants and measurement protocol may differ from yours.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>We removed an earlier illustrative age-band table because it could be mistaken for validated Apple Watch reference ranges. We do not provide a universal “normal” number for a 30-, 40-, 50- or 60-year-old.</p>
<h2>Is SDNN the same as RMSSD?</h2>
<p>No. SDNN describes the spread of normal beat intervals; RMSSD describes successive interval differences. The <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5624990/">review's metric definitions</a> explain the distinction. Do not convert a number from one metric into the other by treating their units as proof of equivalence.</p>
<h2>How do I check my Apple Watch HRV?</h2>
<ol><li>Open Health on your iPhone and search for Heart Rate Variability.</li><li>Inspect the dates, times and data sources behind the displayed values.</li><li>Compare the same metric from the same source over comparable periods.</li><li>Record context such as sleep, exercise and how you felt in a <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">simple observation journal</a>.</li><li>If records are missing, follow the <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting checklist</a>.</li></ol>
<p>Apple describes the measurements collected by its heart sensor in <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">its heart-rate documentation</a>. For the wider distinction between sensor readings and calculated scores, read our <a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch stress guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Is a higher HRV always better?</h2>
<p>No. The <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5624990/">HRV norms review</a> cautions that higher variability is not always a sign of better health. A wearable reading alone cannot establish your health status or explain a change.</p>
<h2>What does Cortisol+ do with HRV?</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ organizes available wearable signals into a wellness score and trend views. The model's baseline rules and missing-data handling are described in <a href="/how-it-works/">our methodology</a>. It does not measure cortisol or establish that a hormone level rose or fell.</p>
<p>Look at the input breakdown and timestamps as well as the summary score. To compare approaches before downloading, use our <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">Apple Watch stress-app guide</a>.</p>
<h2>What if my number looks low?</h2>
<p>A chart or app score cannot determine whether you need medical care. If you have symptoms or are concerned about a persistent change, discuss the readings and their context with a qualified clinician. Do not use HRV to diagnose a cortisol disorder or change prescribed treatment.</p>
